Subject: Reland "Fix heap-buffer-overflow in RunBasedAdditiveBlitter and AAA
 convex walker"

Original change's description:
> Revert "Fix heap-buffer-overflow in RunBasedAdditiveBlitter and AAA convex walker"
>
> This reverts commit 77134dabf84855d57d5bf9f50ee41b61858a48fe.
>
> Reason for revert: Experiment to see if this is blocking the Chrome roll
>
> Failure Link: <LINK TO FAILURE>
>
> Original change's description:
> > Fix heap-buffer-overflow in RunBasedAdditiveBlitter and AAA convex walker
> >
> > When rasterizing a convex path clipped against the right edge of the clip rect:
> > 1. SkEdgeBuilder::combineVertical could cancel opposite-winding vertical edges on the right clip boundary when an X-reversed curve segment separated two same-winding vertical segments. Because convex edge walkers (aaa_walk_convex_edges and walk_simple_edges) do not use winding numbers and require both a left and right edge at every scanline, cancelling opposite-winding vertical edges created a Y-gap with zero edges. Guard opposite-winding vertical cancellation with fCanCullToTheRight.
> > 2. In aaa_walk_convex_edges, when advancing past finished edges across a Y-gap, y was not advanced to std::max({y, leftE->fUpperY, riteE->fUpperY}), causing goY(y) to evaluate the new edge far above its fUpperY and overflow 32-bit signed SkFixed. Advance y (and flush the blitter if y changed) when adopting new edges, and use saturating arithmetic in SkAnalyticEdge::goY.
> > 3. Clamp left, rite, nextLeft, and nextRite to [leftBound, riteBound] across all trapezoid row stages in aaa_walk_convex_edges.
> > 4. Guard against non-positive run lengths (len <= 0 / width <= 0) after left/right clipping in RunBasedAdditiveBlitter::blitAntiH and SafeRLEAdditiveBlitter::blitAntiH before calling SkAlphaRuns::add.
> >
